I am with a company called hello robot  and we'll talk a bit about the robot  we've developed and also a little bit  about some of the prior work I've done  uh first off Stanford Alum very happy to  be back uh it's been an institution uh  dear to my heart that's me back when I  was 18 and first got to campus and I  think that was not long after the Loma  Prieta earthquake so even a lot of the  campus was closed back then  um  and for the talk you know I went back  through my archives and tried to find  some of the old projects I've worked on  and also tried to think about you know a  Common Thread about some of the work  that I've done over the years and so you  can see various humanoids there's a  tensegrity robot there's a biped the  underwater robot with with Asama uh  collaborative robot arms for  manufacturing work with Google analysis  of robots and I think the Common Thread  is really around robots that work around  people and what that means for these  machines to interact with people and so  I want to talk a little bit about that  and I've sort of coined it as being sort  of humanizing robot design uh term I  just made up for this talk but so why  why is that important and for hello  robot in particular  you know we have this this caregiving  crisis that's about to happen across the  world right this is really uh our we  don't have enough people to care for the  older adults uh it really is a crisis  and if you think about someone who's  home alone unable to get out of bed  easily and doing something as simple as  getting up and getting a glass of water  getting some pills it can take a huge  effort and and this is going to be  commonplace this type of problem we  commonplace across the world very soon  and uh so it really is a place where  robots can help and I think we we have  an ability to and that we should help uh  but for today what I want to talk about  is what does it mean to do sort of  human-centric design for for robots  uh and one key aspect for me is really  participatory design which means to put  the stakeholders in the center of the  design process and really talk to them  and involve them so that we know that  what we create is going to meet their  needs and I I hope to highlight some  work that shows why that's so important  um and so to put the human experience at  the center of the design means to really  think about what it means for them to  have this machine in their in their home  and what's that experience for them is  it enjoyable does it bring value to them  is it frustrating and really think that  through carefully rather than building  the technology without an eye towards  that so today I'll talk about kind of  five areas which I think are really  relevant to this one is safety  particularly if we're working with older  vulnerable populations  one is Simplicity and it's very hard to  get to Simplicity but it really matters  in terms of product experience the  experience of the robot  affordability Which is less commonly  researched it's not part of academic  life as much but very important if you  want to have impact you want to get  these robots out in the world and scale  capability for us at hello robot you  know if it's useful it's exciting if  it's capable it's exciting we want these  robots to do meaningful work  and meaningful also means that for the  person it actually enriches their life  it makes their life better that brings  value in a very important way so I'll  highlight how these five areas have kind  of found a thread through my work and  with hello robot  uh let me just start with a quick  introduction to the company  we are based in the Bay Area Up in  Martinez California near Berkeley  uh I'll play this is a about a two  minute video it's a our product launch  video  that's my son Levi who's sitting in the  back there  he just waved  um  [Music]  oh  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  foreign  foreign  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  build it in Martinez California uh sell  it to research Labs internationally it  really is a platform for Innovation so  it's open source lots of great sort of  software apis and we're really  encouraging people to explore the future  of mobile manipulation with this  platform  and we've been seeing great Traction in  the community in terms of adoption  people building machine learning models  on top of it adding new hardware Etc  and uh successful launch we've won some  Design Awards with it a great growing  Community we have them all over the  world at this point and just starting to  see great Publications come out with it  as well  in that hello robot we really do take  seriously the sense of purpose with with  the robots I really believe that these  types of machines can improve life for  everyone and we also recognize this as a  massive undertaking and that we should  embark on this with the whole Community  to try to build this future together  so um let's talk a bit about safety  which I think is  uh it's a very difficult topic and  there's lots of Notions of what safety  is there's this ISO standard for it  which uh is uh really pertinent to a  robot-like stretch but in the end safety  is about  um you know controlling interaction  forces often it's about managing pinch  points Crush points lacerations toppling  and when we're talking about robots with  vulnerable populations we really have to  take it seriously and it's not not a  trivial topic I've done some work over  the years in safety uh notably a lot of  work with serious elastic actuators and  all different types of forms and  variants the main thing about this type  of actuator if you're not familiar it  puts the spring and series with the  gearbox what that does is it decouples  the effect of inertia of the motor and  that gearbox winding up and when it hits  someone it lessens that impact  effectively so that's a really nice  attributes it has some disadvantages of  around complexity uh the fourth  bandwidth position accuracy so there are  trade-offs there but they have found  their way into commercial applications  um you know at my company Mecca we took  the the work that I did at MIT built  humanoids like this one dreamer at UT  Austin uh and then you can see a video  here of one of the humanoids that we  built back in the early 2000s and that  robot everything is serious elastic  actuator including the hands the fingers  it really was fun to interact with very  compliant interactive it felt very  natural  since my company Mecca's found its way  into some commercial robots the Baxter  robot by rethink apptronic has a  humanoid using that technology now  there's actually an exoskeleton for  physical rehab that's using it so really  interesting technology with stretch we  didn't take that path and hopefully it  will illuminate a bit why so first off  for safety you know one thing I've  learned over the years is really you  have to take a systems approach you  can't just solve the component just the  actuator you have to think about the  whole thing so with stretch the thought  about how do we lower the overall Mass  to the manipulator how do we make it so  we're not making these massive uh  actuators to fight gravity so most of  the actuation stretches down on the base  and it really makes out that uh  manipulator to be very lightweight low  inertia makes it safer it's also because  it's a telescoping joint makes it very  intuitive people understand the motion  as it's happening you know a seven-off  arm it can do these complex planning  planned motions and people just don't  know how to interact with that and  becomes unsafe so really with stress we  really took safety as a starting point  from the full systems View and came up  with a very different type of design  uh of course a robot like stretch has  toppling hazards because it is  lightweight but it's got a small  footprint and so some static analysis on  the Tipping stability that we did uh  because it's simple kinematics we get  simple close forms expressions of that  and what we find is we can get  reasonable forces really the biggest tip  tipping Hazard is in in uh pooling can  pull itself over supporting a payload it  does fairly well but you know we can get  modest pulling forces at full height and  full extension and what that means is  that we can actually do reasonable tasks  in households we can't do everything we  can't open a refrigerator door up high  but we can open it down low for example  uh and still with despite all the  thought we put into safety there are  real hazards with a machine like this uh  toppling is is one of them falling down  staircases one so you really have to  still address this this issue in in home  settings  um and also once you get into autonomous  motion it comes very hard to say  anything uh definitively about what what  can happen and so you know we are still  working on how to think about safety  more long term for this product  um move on to simplicity  now this is a robot built at MIT it's my  PhD work a robot named Domo you can see  there all the stuff that I had in it and  dozens of actuators a dozen computers  um  probably five or six different cameras  super complex uh  it was a lot of fun to build it was also  you know very easy to stack up that type  of complexity you know we just bolted  one thing to the next and  um so so complexity is much easier to  get to than Simplicity often so there's  a cool machine but you know I've learned  from that to avoid this type of  complexity  um and one bit of work that came out of  MIT from Rodney Brooks's lab that I  really took inspiration from still do  is this uh this is Herbert it was um  had 8-bit microprocessors he was able to  navigate uh the the offices of the Mita  lab drive around find Coke cans pick  them up and take them back to a location  and it did all that with a very limited  computation and what they did is they  really matched the robot to the  environment they thought carefully about  where do you find Coke cans how do you  recognize those those features with  simple sensors and tune that whole  system to that one task and remarkable  what I could do back then and this  notion of affordances comes out of some  some writing by Don Norman which I  really find inspirational and so with  stretch we took that same approach  and we thought about  you know you know what is a home really  you know do we need to have a general  purpose humanoid in there how can we  design a robot that matches that home  and so homes have lots of flat surfaces  floors countertops table tops  and we thought well a lot of a lot of  what matters happens around flat  surfaces build a system that is really  well designed for that and maybe we can  simplify the overall design problem  so the nice thing that comes from that  things like surface wiping which is a  common task that people would like to  have done in a home becomes much simpler  it's almost trivial to do in this case  so this is autonomous wiping  and you can see a very simple plan  and so what could be a very hard problem  becomes a very simple problem  likewise overall just planning and  manipulation for the robot becomes  simpler one thing we put a lot of  thought into is where to put the camera  it might seem like a fairly simple thing  but we really wanted to give the robot a  good workspace a view of its hand while  it's manipulating a view down while it  was navigating and the the key thing is  you know our environments are built we  look at them from above often and too  often you see robots of the camera down  low looking up and that Viewpoint  doesn't do a give it the right  perspective you need to work in a human  environment and so with this robot  planning manipulation becomes a lot  simpler also because of the Cartesian  kinematics in the placement of that  sensor  uh also you know we really wanted to be  something that felt more like an  appliance you know some of these the  robots that are home robots weigh 400  pounds you need a truck with a lift gate  to move it this I really wanted it to  feel more like a rolling suitcase you  just pick it up and roll it around and  you saw in the video my son was able to  at age eight to just wheel the robot  around so that was a very important  design constraint for us simple to ship  seems very prosaic but the fact that we  can put it in a cardboard box and ship  it by UPS just changes the business that  we have  albeit UPS is terrible with robots we've  had some terrible experiences there  simple to field so you can put in the  back of a car take it to a home  experiment you know the fact that you  can throw it I put in back my Prius all  the time it's just it changes what the  robot is and how you interact with it  um so that's uh Simplicity talk about  affordability again the lesson I've  gathered in getting to low cost is it  really is a systems problem  this pie chart is sort of the the cost  of of stretch as you can see it's not  one one pie wedge is is like the  dominant cost you really have to solve  all these different pie wedges  um and also there's all these hidden  costs right so the shipping cost the  warranty cost all the packaging for the  robot it just adds up and so if you're  building a low-cost product you can't  just focus on you know the actuator or  the sensor you have to think about the  whole thing  uh some some of the first work I did in  low-cost with with Professor kakoski  here at Stanford SRI this is a low-cost  hand that was done for DARPA really cool  technology packed in there so I had  Twisted string actuators so a little  piece of string you'd wind up like an  airplane rubber band it would pull on  the tendon each finger had a  electrostatic clutch so with one motor  you could do three degrees of freedom in  each finger  tactile skin capacitive encoded on the  on the fingers so super cool hand  unfortunately it didn't make it past  this prototype never got commercialized  but we we did learn a lot about kind of  developing low-cost Technologies  um but here's another take on a low-cost  hand so this is the stretch hand  and what we did is we let Amazon  crowdsource the the design by you know  we look at all the assistive Grabbers on  Amazon  you know looked at the highest rated one  you know this had thousands of reviews  said well this one must work pretty well  and so we took that added a dynamixel  Servo except for eighty dollars in  quantity one we have a we have a gripper  and it works remarkably well by the way  so  a a later project I had was a company  called Redwood robotics we're doing  collaborative robot arms you can see  here it's Force sensitive uh and this  was it was a full low-cost arm sheet  metal and belts timing belts uh strain  gauges from China that were five dollars  each uh and it worked really well we got  good Precision out of it good Force  sensitivity uh a little bit ugly in this  prototype  but um you know we're really going down  the path of can we get to a very  low-cost collaborative arm for  manufacturing and you know around the  time we were about to start  commercializing we got bought by Google  so that turned into  a project at Google that I that I led  which is these robots here that's that's  our Redwood arm it's an arm Farm they're  doing machine learning uh reinforcement  learning with the arm loading to grasp  objects  um that project unfortunately got got  shut down so we never got to the light  of day with that but but I ended up  spinning up a project that became  Everyday Robots which is a mobile  manipulation project  and you know one lesson there was that  well you can make a low-cost arm it's  not the same as a low-cost uh system  low-cost mobile manipulator and the arm  is just one component of that box so  you know with the arm we're very focused  on the transmission and getting that  cost down getting rid of harmonic drives  for example but really the whole robot  has you know so many sensors and compute  and the total cost that those gearboxes  are small in comparison  um so really you know again systems  design is what with stretch gave us a  significant cost reduction so far lower  degrees of freedom because of the  kinematic Arrangement and you can see  you know we're down here we saw it for  about twenty thousand dollars with a  good profit which means our bomb cost is  a very low because we really took the  systems approach seriously uh and in  comparison you know most other robots 5x  10x more expensive  talk a bit about capability we really  want it stretch to be useful for people  in homes we did a lot of testing a lot  of just eyes on tell operation of the  robot in homes cleaning toilets wiping  surfaces picking up toys playing games  with kids just to verify that the robot  could actually do it even with tele  operation and this is in comparison to  other manipulation platforms that are  super interesting technology but just to  me aren't credible to be in the home  anytime in the future  uh and so you know when we start at  stretch we're inspired by the PR2 which  is a fantastic platform  but you know it's very large uh it's  about 500 pounds 400 000 and it just  wasn't going to be a credible robot for  the home the thing is you can't just  shrink that robot right it doesn't won't  be a capable robot and so uh rather than  shrinking it we had to kind of rethink  what it meant for it to have our home  robot and what we came up with we can  call robot cubism right you take the  intent of a human but you're not  building a human exactly so it's sort of  a deconstructed robot we put it back  together with the systems approach in  mind  and some of the characteristics are  first off it has the reach of roughly of  a 50 percentile uh male so very good arm  reach  and by the way it scales very well so if  we made a bigger version because of that  telescoping arm for every you know inch  you add in the base width you get about  three and a half inches in reach so a  bi